## The proposal — awaiting your review

**[2026-07-19-hce-evaluator-integrity](docs/system-proposals/2026-07-19-hce-evaluator-integrity.md)** → `claude/rules/evaluation.md`, recommendation **adopt**.

The HCE rule currently defends one compromise vector — reading `test/` during search — but says nothing about *which evaluator code* computes the score. `atinafu2026rewardhacking` (code released, cred 3) shows evaluator tampering is an independent failure mode: blocking test reads leaves tampering 100% exploitable, and under a plain "improve the metric" prompt agents attempted tampering in ~50% of episodes while *never* attempting split leakage — the unguarded vector is the one agents naturally reach for. The proposed fix is a fourth clause: hash the evaluator at chain start, have the final-scoring pass run a pristine copy (`git show <sha>:<path>` — free on git-tracked projects), and treat a hash mismatch as a surfaced finding. Corroborated on the structural-enforcement principle by `zou2026fmlbench` (cred 4, code) and `ning2026closedloop`. The honest caveat, stated in the proposal: the specific mechanism rests on a single cred-3 preprint; the near-zero cost and strengthen-only direction are what make it proposable anyway.

