## Proposed: [`2026-08-23-elevate-paired-control`](docs/system-proposals/2026-08-23-elevate-paired-control.md) → `skills/elevate/SKILL.md`

`refusal-cost-symmetry` (seeded 08-18, 5 sources) names this skill explicitly: `/elevate`'s two gates are *both* permissive-failure guards. Nothing measures a good idea held back — so a genuinely quiet cycle and a gate that has drifted into rejecting everything produce byte-identical output, since a block-all gate writes excellent hold notes.

`ho2026soundnessbench` (cred 4, dataset released) measures exactly this on research-proposal triage: an aggressive prompt cuts false approvals 74.0→19.9% while good-proposal recall collapses 91.8→36.1% and **Macro F1 falls** 54.9→49.3; two models sit at 0% false approvals with ~0% recall. `ray2026what` reaches the same block-all endpoint independently — conformal calibration returns block-all for all 23 judges tested.

The fix is `tripathi2026diagnostic`'s paired legitimate case in its cheapest form: each cycle, re-test one already-accepted proposal against today's gates (rotating oldest-first) and log `control=<slug>:<pass|fail>`. ~10 lines in one existing file, no new file/skill/hook/knob, and the control set is a free by-product of the skill's own operation. Stated limitation in the proposal: those seven proposals were written by this same gate, so it detects **drift**, not initial mis-calibration.

